**Locked Safe minigame (first-person, tactile).** The left hand holds the tension wrench (hold RMB); the right hand sweeps the pick (mouse X, 0–180°). Locks have 3 / 4 / 5 / 6 pins by grade (1–4), set in a hidden random order, with sweet-spot widths of ±12° / ±9° / ±6° / ±4°. Feedback is audio-first: ticks sharpen near the sweet spot, plus controller haptics and a subtle wrench-angle cue, with no UI bar. Stress builds while tension is applied off-spot (`rate = 6 + 14 × offset`); the pick snaps at its durability (Basic 40, Hardened 90, Master Set 200), and a snapped pick resets set pins. Alternatives trade risk for speed: **code note** (found in the structure; no pick needed), **thermal lance** (torch, 25 s; each T3+ item has a 25% chance to be destroyed; high noise), **explosive** (50% of contents destroyed; 80 m noise).

**Loot Budget allocation (per structure, deterministic).**

1. Budget (LBP) = base × difficulty ring × party × night. Bases: Dam 140, Yard 110, Tunnel 90, Factory 180, Wreckline 60, Spire 50. Ring: +12% per 50 km, cap ×2.0. Party: 1P ×0.7, 2P ×0.85, 3P ×1.0, 4P ×1.15. Night visit: ×1.5.
2. Reserve 40 LBP if the structure has a T4-eligible container (Vault Cache or grade 3+ Safe) and the pity rule fires.
3. Split the rest across containers by weight (Scrap 1, Crate 3, Safe 4, Vault 8).
4. Each container rolls items until its share or its roll count is exhausted, biasing 40% toward the structure’s affinity category (Dam: electrical; Yard: mechanical and fuel; Tunnel: mining and explosives; Factory: assemblies).
5. Leftover LBP converts to T1 scrap.

**T4 pity timer.** The counter increases by `dangerRating × 4` per cleared structure; at ≥ 100 the next eligible structure is forced to hold a blueprint fragment and the counter resets. Pacing target: one full blueprint per 90–120 km if players clear at least 60% of Tier 3+ structures.

**Co-op rules.** Contents roll lazily on first open, server-side, from `ContainerSeed`, so all players see the same loot. Carry classes: Light (under 1 kg, pockets), Medium (1–5 kg, backpack), Heavy (5–25 kg, two-hand carry, no sprint), Oversize (25–60 kg, two-player carry, hand truck or winch).

## 2. Night Mechanics & the Away-From-Train Threat

### 2.1 Day/Night Cycle & Atmosphere Director

A single scalar `t ∈ [0,1)` (time of day) drives animation curves for sun and moon elevation, color temperature, exposure, ambient SH, height-fog density, volumetric scattering, shadow distance, LUT blend and audio mix. Default cycle: 30 minutes.

| Phase | Length | Sun / moon | Fog density · outside visibility | Look | Audio bed | Threat state |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Dawn | 2:00 | Sun rises, 3,200 → 5,200 K | 0.0035 · mist burns off over 90 s | Warm lift, long shadows | Birds return, wind drops, music swells | Forgotten dissolve |
| Day | 16:00 | Sun arc, 5,600 K | 0.0006 · \~3 km | Saturated, full GI | Birds, insects, grass wind, rail clatter, soft acoustic score | None |
| Dusk | 3:00 | Sunset, 6,500 → 2,800 K | 0.0015 → 0.003 | Orange to violet; shadow distance −40% | Birds fade over 90 s, crickets start, music thins to a pad | Prep window: lamps auto-ignite, Perimeter self-test |
| Early Night | 3:00 | Moon at 35°, 7,500 K | 0.006 · \~180 m | Saturation −25%, blue-teal LUT; local lights dominate | Night bed: distant calls, creaks; music off | ACTIVE for exposed players (Build-up) |
| Deep Night | 4:00 | Moon high or overcast | 0.012 · \~90 m | Saturation −45%, vignette; the flashlight is the only reliable sight | 32–40 Hz sub-drone, perimeter hum, false-cue rustles | Peak |
| Pre-Dawn | 2:00 | Sky brightens from 1:30 | 0.008 and falling | Cold grey-blue | Wind rises, dread bed thins, first bird | Relax, then dissolve |

**Per-player dread blending (client-side).** The Night Director replicates only an `ExposureLevel` (0–1) per player. Each client blends it into: fog density ×(1 + 1.5 × E), draw distance −35%, ambient bed −12 dB, film grain +20%, low-pass cutoff 20 kHz → 8 kHz. Players inside the perimeter see none of it.

**The Perimeter Clear Pocket.** A capsule SDF follows the train as a volumetric-fog mask: density ×0.3 inside, warm local lighting, and a soft heat-shimmer fog wall at 15 m. Outside it, saturation lerps 1.0 → 0.55 across 15–25 m. The emitter’s 60 Hz hum rolls off from 12 m and cuts completely at 15 m, the ‘held breath’ moment.

**Exposure Escalation Ladder** (per exposed player; timers run at `ExposureRate = 1 + d/40`, where `d` is meters beyond the perimeter):

| Time since crossing | Beat | System response |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 s | Cross 15 m | Hum cuts, HUD ring flickers, heartbeat bed starts |
| 3 s | Notice | Ambient −6 dB; Director spawn lock released |
| 8 s | First contact | 2–3 Shamblers spawn 40–70 m out, unseen |
| 20 s | Shadow | Stalker begins Shadow Tail (if unlocked) |
| 45 s | Swarm | Crawler pack (6–14) enters Search |
| 90 s, or `d` over 80 m | Elite | Hollowed Brute check |

### 2.2 The Train Safe Zone (Radio/Thermal Perimeter)

**Field definition.** `dist(p)` is the minimum over all cars and exterior blocks of `SDF_OBB(p, block)`, evaluated in train-local space at 10 Hz per player. The visual boundary sits at 15.0 m; the logical exit at 15.5 m and re-entry at 15.0 m (hysteresis prevents edge flicker). A player standing on the train is always inside.

**Emitter power.** The Perimeter Emitter block draws 4 PU/min from the battery bank (charged by a boiler dynamo). Radius degrades with output, so maintenance becomes survival:

| Emitter output | Radius | Typical cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 100–60% | 15 m | Normal |
| 59–30% | 10 m | Low boiler pressure, dynamo wear |
| 29–1% | 5 m | Empty tender, flooded battery car |
| 0% | 0 m (train unprotected) | Breaker trip, destroyed emitter |

**Peace rules (hard constraints).**

1. The Night Director tracks only exposed players; safe players have Director budget 0.
2. No mob may hold a target inside the field; target validity requires attacker and victim both outside.
3. Mob positions are clamped to at least radius + 1 m; in-flight attacks that would land inside are cancelled.
4. Spawn exclusion: no spawns within 25 m of any hull, or in line of sight of any player.
5. Standoff: mobs that lose their target ring at 16–22 m and pace for 20 s (silhouettes visible through the fog wall), then Ebb into the dark.

**Night driving.** Allowed, and mob-free inside the field. Tension comes from 90–180 m visibility, headlamp range (60 / 120 / 200 m by lamp tier) and one environmental Rail Hazard per \~6 km (washout, fallen tree, rockslide). **Stations** project a permanent 150 m sanctuary.

### 2.3 Night Mobs: The Forgotten

Three core mobs attack three separate stealth axes (light, shadow, sound), so no single counter-strategy covers all of them.

| Mob | Role | HP | Speed (m/s) | Senses | Counterplay | Unlocks |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Shambler | Light-drawn grunt | 120 | Walk 1.6, run 3.2 | Sight 25 m (110° cone); hearing 12 m; drawn to the brightest light within 40 m | Fire (burn DoT), headshot ×2, leg hit staggers; slower than a sprint | Night 1 |
| Blind Crawler | Sound-hunting swarm | 45 each (pack of 6–14) | Scuttle 4.5; climbs walls | No sight; hearing 30 m; ground vibration | Silence (crouch, soft surfaces), noise decoys, fire; light is irrelevant | Night 2 |
| Stalker | Lightbreaker ambusher | 70 | Prowl 2.2, dash 8.5 (1.2 s, 6 s cooldown) | Night vision 45 m; hearing 20 m | Back-to-back stance, burning flare (3 m no-entry), spare bulbs and glow sticks | Night 3 |
| Wailer | Alarm support | 80 | 3.0 | Sight 30 m | Priority target; its howl adds +20% budget and calls Shamblers within 80 m | Night 4 |
| Hollowed Brute | Elite | 900 | Walk 2.0, charge 6.5 | Sight 20 m; hearing 15 m | Stagger-immune; dodge the charge into a wall (3 s stun); fire and explosives | Night 6 |

**State machines (HFSM).**

- **Shambler:** Spawned → SeekLight → Chase → Attack (1.4 s wind-up, 18 dmg) → Stagger → Standoff → Dissolve.
- **Blind Crawler:** Burrow-out → Listen (clinging to walls) → Investigate (speed scales with noise loudness) → Search (6 s spiral) → SwarmAttack (6 dmg per bite) → Scatter (on fire).
- **Stalker:** Spawned → ShadowTail (stays 15–25 m outside light cones, flanks via the Darkness influence layer) → Isolate (waits until the target faces away or the group splits) → Lunge (aims at the held or head-mounted light) → Smash (destroys the flashlight, 12 dmg; with no light, 25 dmg plus stagger) → Withdraw (8–15 s) → loop; after 3 lunges, Retreat for 40 s.
- **Wailer:** Lurk (40–60 m) → Howl (every 25 s) → Flee (target within 20 m).
- **Brute:** Stalk → Charge (1.2 s telegraph, 45 dmg, breaks barricades) → Recover.

**Perception model.** Sight (cone plus occlusion rays), Hearing (noise events with a loudness radius), Light (illuminance at the mob), Vibration (surface impacts). Base noise radius: Crouch 2 m, Walk 6 m, Sprint 14 m, Jump landing 8 m, Door slam 15 m, Lockpick 3 m, Crowbar 20 m, Gunshot 60 m (suppressed 25 m), multiplied by the surface noise factor in section 3.3.

**NavMesh and AI architecture.**

- Tiled NavMesh (64 m tiles) streams with terrain. Three agent profiles: Humanoid (r 0.4, h 1.8), Crawler (r 0.25, h 0.5, wall and ceiling Climb links), Brute (r 0.9, h 2.8).
- Cost layers: Perimeter (impassable inside the field), Light (+4 for Stalkers, −2 for Shamblers), Noise Memory (heat map that decays over 30 s).
- The Train NavMesh Surface is pre-baked per car in local space, stitched at couplers and rebuilt asynchronously (≤ 100 ms) when blocks change. Only Pursuit boarders use it.
- Caps: 14 active mobs per exposed player, 36 server-wide; AI LOD drops avoidance beyond 80 m and despawns out-of-sight mobs beyond 120 m.

**Night Director (macro layer).** Per exposed player: `budget += rate × ExposureRate × partyScale × nightScale` each second. Costs: Crawler 1, Shambler 2, Stalker 6, Wailer 8, Brute 25. Spawn query: sample NavMesh points in a 35–70 m ring and reject any in the frustum or in line of sight (head and feet rays), within 25 m of a hull, or within 6 m of another spawn; prefer terrain-occluded cells and Night Nest tags. Pacing loop: Build-up (0–45 s) → Sustain (45–120 s) → Peak (20 s spike) → Relax (12–20 s enforced lull), repeating.

