**Implementation.** Per-chunk depth textures (R16, 0.5 m texels) hold snow, mud and water depth, sampled under each foot. `speedMult = lerp(1.0, 0.35, saturate(depth / 0.8))`; stamina multiplier `1 + 1.2 × depthRatio`. Footprints stamp into a deformation render texture, and AI `TrailMemory` lets Shamblers track fresh prints for up to 60 s. Biome variants: snow muffles Shambler steps (a hearing counter-play), swamp adds Wet and slow, desert dune slopes slide above 30°.

### 3.4 Tactile Interaction Framework

**Targeting.** A 3 m look ray plus a 0.15 m sphere cast; the primary target is the highest score `0.6 × alignment + 0.3 × proximity + 0.1 × context`. A **Hand Proxy** (kinematic body) mediates all physics interactions.

| Verb | Input | Implementation | Feedback | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TAP | E press | Event | Click, animation | Hatch latch, furnace door, switch |
| HOLD | E hold, 0.4–3 s | Progress ring | Tool audio | Prying a crate, relighting a lamp |
| DRAG | Hold E + mouse axis | Hinge or slider joint driven by Hand Proxy force | Detent clicks, haptics, live gauge response | Levers |
| ROTATE | Hold E + circular mouse | Rotational drive, 15° detents | Squeal, resistance | Valves, hatch wheels |
| GRAB | Hold G | Spring joint, max force from mass | Weight sway | Coal chunks, planks, tools |
| INSERT | Item + E | Socket snap | Click | Fuse, gas canister into torch |
| USE | LMB hold with tool | Tool simulation | Tool FX | Welding torch, hammer, rake |

**Physics levers.** A lever is a hinge joint with limits (throttle 0–60°, brake 0–90°), damping and optional spring return. The Hand Proxy applies `F = k × (target − current)` clamped to `maxPull` (default 120 N); heavy levers (turntable crank, emergency brake) need two players. Notches are magnetic torque wells (reverser at −1 / 0 / +1). Output `value ∈ [0,1]` is sampled at 50 Hz by the train subsystem. Assist mode: click to set a notch. Network: the first grabber holds an Authority Lock; others add force but are clamped; the value replicates at 20 Hz while held and the server freezes it on release.

**Locomotive cab controls (E1 baseline).**

| Control | Type | Range | Effect | Failure mode |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Regulator | Drag lever | 0–100% | Steam admission, traction | Wheel slip above 85% on wet rail (Sander fixes) |
| Reverser | Notched drag | −1 to +1, cut-off 15–75% | Direction, efficiency | Reversing at speed damages valve gear |
| Auto Brake | Drag, 4 notches | Release / Hold / Service / Emergency | Brake-pipe pressure | Leaky hoses lengthen stopping distance |
| Independent Brake | Drag | 0–100% | Engine-only brake | Overuse causes wheel flats |
| Blower | Rotate valve | 0–100% | Draft when stopped | Flashback if the firebox door opens without it |
| Injectors L/R | Rotate + tap | On/off, feed rate | Water into boiler | Lose prime on steam/water mismatch (gurgle cue) |
| Damper | Drag slider | 0–100% | Air supply | Over-draft wastes fuel; under-draft smokes |
| Firebox door | TAP | Open/close | Access, heat flash | Backdraft burst: 15 dmg |
| Whistle cord | Drag pull | 0–1 | Signals, warnings | — |
| Emitter breaker | Switch | On/off | Perimeter power | Trips above 110% load |

**Firebox and boiler simulation.** The firebox is an 8 × 5 cell grid; each cell stores `fuelMass`, `heat` and `clinker`. Steam generation is `Q = Σ heat × transfer(1 − clinker)`; pressure changes by `dP/dt = (Q − demand − leak) / boilerVolume`. Safe band 8–12 bar, relief valve at 13, rupture risk above 14 bar for 5 s. Water below 25% of the gauge glass for 8 s triggers a **Boiler Failure Cascade** (scalding steam, power loss, engine disabled until 3 T3 parts restore it).

- **Shoveling:** a scoop (LMB at the tender pile, 0.8 s) carries 4 chunks. Aim at the 0.4 × 0.3 m door aperture and release; chunks fly as rigid bodies and land in cells by trajectory. Even coverage (at least 80% of cells fueled) gives +10% efficiency; hot spots above 1.4 × the mean wear the flue sheet.
- **Single chunks:** GRAB one by hand (two per hand); loose coal rolls with train motion. The tender shows up to 150 chunks as physical instances; the rest is an abstract volume.
- **Clinker:** rake across the grate (drag gesture) to remove it, then dump the ash pan by lever.
- **Furnace door ritual:** TAP opens the door (0.6 s animation, heat flash if the player stands in front for over 3 s). Opening it without the Blower on triggers a Backdraft.

| Fuel | Heat units per chunk | Burn time | Ignition | Clinker per chunk |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Wood log | 40 | 25 s | Fast | 0.02 |
| Coal chunk | 100 | 60 s | Medium | 0.08 |
| Anthracite | 140 | 75 s | Slow | 0.02 |
| Fuel-oil canister (BP-T and BP-X only) | 260 | 90 s | Instant | 0 |

Baseline burn at cruise (E1, 40 km/h) is about 10 chunks/min, so a 400-chunk tender covers \~27 km. That is why Fuel Caches appear every 12 km and Stations refuel fully.

**Welding torch and hull repair.** Each car’s hull is a grid of 1 × 1 m panels with Integrity 0–100.

| Integrity | State | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 80–100 | Pristine | None |
| 50–79 | Dented | Cosmetic, slight drag |
| 20–49 | Cracked | Heat loss, weather exposure |
| 1–19 | Breached | Water ingress; boarders can enter during the Pursuit |
| 0 | Destroyed | Open gap |

Repair flow: scrape rust (wire brush, HOLD) → place patch plate (steel plate, snaps to the breach) → tack four corners (TAP × 4) → seam weld (USE along the glowing Weld Path) → optional quench. **Quick-Patch** (plank + nails, 4 s) caps integrity at 40%. The torch burns a gas canister (120 s of flame) or a battery (arc welder, T3). Weld Quality is `100 × (0.5·speed + 0.3·path + 0.2·standoff)`, each term scored 0–1: target travel 4 ± 1.5 cm/s, path error under 1 cm, standoff 5–9 cm. Results: under 40 burns through (−10 integrity, retry), 40–69 restores to 70%, 70–89 restores to 90%, 90 or more restores to 100% plus rust resistance. Goggles are required; without them the screen whites out for 3 s. Sparks ignite spilled oil (Fire System).

